+# OLED Aegis Windhawk V1 development plan
+
+This plan turns the operator's direction in [D-0003](../../state/DECISIONS.md) into the V1 implementation sequence and release criteria accepted in D-0004. Its audience is the operator and the developer implementing the Windhawk edition. The baseline is a new Windhawk-native structure, with narrowly justified reuse of reviewed implementation details. The existing application supplies behavioral requirements and lessons from its defects.
+
+Feasibility statements below distinguish documented mechanisms from demonstrated behavior. Phase progress belongs in [LOOP-015](../../state/OPEN_LOOPS.md); this document is the authoritative home for the design baseline and acceptance criteria. The [production handoff](../prompts/windhawk-v1-production-handoff.md) is the active execution entry point. D-0007 governs the revised validation policy below.
+
+## Validation policy for production work
+
+Hands-on testing is unavailable. Under D-0007, proceed with production implementation and available automated verification without waiting for physical-desktop qualification. The Phase 1 report remains an accurate historical result; its unpassed multi-display gate is no longer an implementation prerequisite.
+
+All feature requirements remain in scope. Record implementation status separately from test evidence. Simulated topology, injected input, and harness checks can verify policy and selected platform mechanisms but do not prove visible rendering, physical input behavior, or Windhawk lifecycle on the normal desktop. Those observations remain deferred under LOOP-016.
+
+The phase gates below continue to describe the intended qualification standard. Where they require unavailable hands-on or hardware evidence, record the gap and continue production work using the revised acceptance policy. This overrides wording that otherwise stops Phase 2 until actual multi-display testing passes. Available automated checks and defects found by those checks still require resolution; unavailable tests are not a pass.
+
+Deliver a production V1 candidate with all requested behavior implemented, available automated checks passing, attribution and package documentation prepared, and remaining qualification explicit. Use "implementation complete with desktop qualification outstanding" where appropriate. Full runtime qualification and a supported stable-release claim remain separate from that delivery.
+
+## Evidence and feasibility
+
+The inherited behavior reference is [README.md](../../../README.md) and [src/oled_aegis.c](../../../src/oled_aegis.c) at commit `9581aa1ea9e297c1c8e0b1f113c84ab215611ce1`. The local independent assessment is [the audit report](../../../audit-reports/audit-2026-10-04-9581aa1.md). That report is ignored local evidence; preserve an appropriate report or findings record for collaborators before relying on its availability elsewhere. Supported standalone compilation and desktop runtime behavior remain unverified. See [LOOP-002 and LOOP-004](../../state/OPEN_LOOPS.md).
+
+| Question | Evidence | Planning conclusion |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Can Windhawk host a persistent utility? | Its [tool-mod documentation](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk/wiki/Mods-as-tools:-Running-mods-in-a-dedicated-process) describes a dedicated host and lifecycle callbacks. | Use a dedicated user-level host. The mod owns its UI loop and cleanup. There is no need for an OLED Aegis executable distributed separately. |
+| Can it retain a tray icon? | [Monitor Sleep Button](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk-mods/blob/main/mods/monitor-sleep-button.wh.cpp) already combines a tray icon, menu, black window, and shell-restart recovery. | Tray parity is feasible. The example establishes capability, not correctness of any copied cleanup code. |
+| Is preview hosting a practical screensaver approach? | [DisplayFusion documents](https://www.displayfusion.com/HelpGuide/ScreenSaverDevelopment/) passing `/p` and a parent window handle, and requires correct preview sizing. [Actual Tools documents](https://www.actualtools.com/windowmanager/help/features/multi_monitor_screen_saver.php) preview-window compatibility as a prerequisite. | Use preview-compatible savers as the initial compatibility class. These products are precedents, not evidence that this implementation already works. |
+| Does that prove independent activity handling? | Separate per-display selection does not establish independent idle timers, dismissal, focus, or media behavior. | Prove monitor A stays usable while monitor B runs a saver. This is the main early technical gate. |
+| Can every `.scr` be supported? | The documented [Windows command line](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/troubleshoot/windows/win32/screen-saver-command-line) exposes run and preview modes, without a general monitor-selection parameter. Actual Tools explicitly limits compatibility. | Publish a tested compatibility list and explicit failures. Universal compatibility is outside the V1 promise. |
+
+The Windhawk documentation currently describes a wrapper for 1.7.3 and built-in tool support in 2.0 alpha. Proposed default: target the supported stable release available when implementation starts, and keep newer-host compatibility without requiring an alpha. Verify the installed compiler and host versions in Phase 1. Do not assume the earlier failed standalone C syntax probe proves the new C++ mod cannot compile.
+
+## V1 product contract
+
+V1 implementation must cover all existing intended capabilities and the new independent screensaver capability. Record each acceptance-matrix result under the revised validation policy; fully qualified V1 still requires the actual runtime evidence. An internal black-overlay milestone is not a complete V1 implementation.
+
+Each display has an enabled flag and a presentation choice: black or a selected stock Windows screensaver. Black remains the default. Each display uses the global timeout initially; separate timeout overrides are an optional later enhancement. Global input mode and per-monitor input mode remain available. One display can show a saver while another shows black and a third remains in use.
+
+The normal workflow is to install the mod, enable desired monitors, choose their presentation from available stock savers, preview each selection, and continue using the desktop. A tray icon provides manual start/stop, settings, and a session pause. A settings window may supplement Windhawk settings for monitor selection and saver configuration; both must operate on one configuration model. General third-party file selection is a later extension.
+
+Stock savers remain executable programs launched separately from the host. V1 discovers the installed Windows copies and does not bundle or automatically download them. Selecting a saver records a preference; an explicit preview or ordinary enabled idle activation runs it. Animation is a user preference, not a claim of equal OLED protection to black output. Normal Windows display sleep remains effective; the mod must not continuously wake a display to keep an animation running.
+
+### Existing capability acceptance matrix
+
+| ID | Required capability | V1 acceptance evidence |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| P01 | Per-monitor enablement and persistent identity | Mixed and all-disabled selections survive restart, reordered displays, disconnection, and reconnection. Retain disconnected-monitor settings. Define behavior for ambiguous identical displays and cloned output. |
+| P02 | Global idle mode | Enabled eligible monitors activate after the configured timeout and dismiss on activity. Verify the existing timeout range of 5 to 3600 seconds. |
+| P03 | Per-monitor idle mode | Activity attributed to A keeps A usable without resetting B. Movement onto B dismisses B. Keyboard attribution and cursor behavior match the documented policy below. |
+| P04 | Polling configuration | Preserve the 250 to 10000 ms range and default. Input between polls is not lost merely because it is older than a fixed freshness window. Activation latency is bounded by the configured check interval plus measured scheduling tolerance. |
+| P05 | Media suppression controls | Global/per-monitor modes and the muted-media option remain configurable. Verify playing, paused, muted, inaudible, ended, moved, inaccessible, and exited player cases. Publish browser and endpoint limitations. |
+| P06 | Black presentation and edge padding | Cover the selected monitor correctly at mixed DPI and negative desktop coordinates. Preserve the padding setting and range. Show its effect on adjacent displays; do not silently change its meaning. |
+| P07 | Tray and manual controls | Tray icon, menu, settings access, manual start/stop, and temporary exit/pause work. Any-running state is derived from monitor states. Icon returns after Explorer restarts. |
+| P08 | Durable settings and diagnostics | Validate/clamp settings, show persistence failures, preserve all-disabled configuration, and keep optional logging bounded and nonfatal. Diagnostic output is off by default. |
+| P09 | Startup behavior | Document the dependency on Windhawk starting with Windows. Preserve an explicit per-mod automatic-activation preference; do not silently change Windhawk's global startup choice or keep an obsolete OLED Aegis Run entry. Explain the adapted UX. |
+| P10 | Resume and topology recovery | Resume, display changes, DPI changes, and default audio-device changes refresh relevant state without orphan windows or unexpected immediate activation. |
+| P11 | Existing configuration transition | Offer a deliberate, validated import of recognized legacy INI settings into the new model. Leave the original file intact. Explain startup/log-path changes and unsupported keys. |
+
+The intended input contract is the legacy documented user experience, with defects corrected: mouse activity credits the cursor display; keyboard activity credits the foreground-window display and cursor display. The legacy implementation can over-credit both displays because it does not reliably distinguish input kinds. Phase 1 must establish whether the new implementation can meet the intended policy without swallowing or altering input. Polling alone must not be described as exact event attribution. When input cannot be attributed after a missed interval or topology change, conservatively reset affected timers rather than pretend the observation was exact.
+
+Per-monitor media matching is also heuristic. The current implementation's process-name and title matching cannot establish exact browser-tab ownership. V1 must preserve useful controls and publish tested limitations; it must not advertise perfect attribution. Default-device-only coverage is the minimum legacy baseline; broader endpoint support is an improvement if validated, not an assumed property.
+
+### New screensaver acceptance matrix
+
+| ID | Required capability | V1 acceptance evidence |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| S01 | Choose a saver for each monitor | Different compatible savers run simultaneously on A and B; black remains usable on C. Saving and reloading preserves assignments. |
+| S02 | Independent activation and dismissal | A remains interactive while B runs. B activity dismisses B without unnecessarily terminating C. Typing on A, media keys, focus changes, and cursor crossings follow the documented activity policy. |
+| S03 | Preview and configuration | Explicit preview targets the selected monitor and can always be stopped. Provide a configure action for a saver that implements the conventional configuration interface. Do not promise separate preferences for each instance when a saver stores global settings. |
+| S04 | Compatibility qualification | Test every available stock Windows candidate listed below, same-saver multiple instances, and expected incompatibilities. Every published entry identifies file version/hash, architecture, Windows build, and observed limitations. No untested saver is labelled supported. |
+| S05 | Failure recovery | Missing file, launch error, hung startup, early exit, and crash leave a controllable black fallback on the affected idle monitor, with a concise diagnostic. No endless automatic relaunch loop. |
+| S06 | Resource cleanup | Stop, disable, unload, sleep, session lock, monitor removal, and host termination leave no owned saver processes/windows behind. Verify the process-tree containment approach experimentally. |
+| S07 | Focus and bounds | Accepted savers stay within their host and do not steal focus, consume input intended for another monitor, or force global dismissal. Reject or document incompatible behavior rather than patch arbitrary saver binaries. |
+| S08 | Power and resources | Display power-off still works; suspended/locked sessions stop presentation work. Measure host and child CPU/GPU/memory separately and publish reference-system results. |
+
+V1 excludes secure-desktop or per-monitor lock-screen replacement, arbitrary fullscreen saver reparenting, a universal `.scr` guarantee, third-party saver qualification, a new animation engine, wallpaper/video playback, spanning a single animation across the desktop, a saver marketplace, and full DisplayFusion/Actual Multiple Monitors feature parity. These are proposed scope boundaries, not claims of platform impossibility.
+
+### Stock Windows compatibility targets
+
+The operator selected stock Windows savers as the initial target. The following executable names are candidates, not validated support claims. Availability and behavior must be recorded for each supported Windows build; V1 must not download replacements for absent components.
+
+| Saver | Executable | Qualification emphasis |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Bubbles | `Bubbles.scr` | Preview background/transparency, full-size output, focus, independent dismissal. |
+| Mystify | `Mystify.scr` | Preview scaling, continuous rendering, two simultaneous instances. |
+| Ribbons | `Ribbons.scr` | Preview scaling, GPU load, repeated start/stop. |
+| 3D Text | `ssText3d.scr` | Configuration UI, global versus per-instance settings, graphics lifecycle. |
+| Photos | `PhotoScreensaver.scr` | Configuration, valid/empty/missing photo sources, preview support, any child processes. |
+| Blank | `scrnsave.scr` | Preview-host control case and cleanup; the native black renderer remains the normal blank option. |
+
+For each candidate, record pass, limited support, unsupported, or unavailable, with evidence. Test all available candidates before release. At least two animated stock savers must pass the Phase 1 gate to justify the approach; passing two is not permission to claim all stock savers work. Any limitation that removes a requested stock capability must be surfaced before finalizing the V1 compatibility promise.
+
+## Proposed architecture
+
+Keep the implementation small, with explicit ownership even if Windhawk distribution uses one `.wh.cpp` file. Start with logical sections; introduce source assembly only if size justifies it, and keep any generated distributable reproducible and reviewable. Windhawk's [mod format and lifecycle APIs](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk/wiki/Creating-a-new-mod) determine packaging and integration.
+
+| Component | Responsibility |
+| --- | --- |
+| Windhawk adapter | Initialization, settings notifications, user-session singleton, and complete shutdown in the dedicated host. |
+| Configuration | One validated/versioned model for general preferences, per-monitor choices, and explicit legacy import. |
+| Monitor catalog | Stable identities, topology snapshots, bounds, DPI, and disconnected settings. |
+| Activity and media observation | Produce timestamped observations. Use monotonic elapsed time; keep slow media/process queries off the UI path when necessary. |
+| Policy controller | Decide per-monitor states: disabled, active desktop, idle but suppressed, black, external saver, or fallback. Treat pause/suspend as explicit overriding conditions. |
+| Presentation | Separate black-window and external-saver implementations behind start/stop/resize operations. |
+| Tray and settings UI | Present controller state and send commands through the same model. Do not maintain a competing global active flag. |
+
+The controller owns one state record per monitor. A start operation creates a generation identifier so late asynchronous results cannot revive an old monitor session. Shutdown stops scheduling, cancels outstanding work, closes owned saver processes, destroys windows/tray resources on their owner thread, and joins workers before the mod unloads.
+
+For an external saver, create a correctly scaled host window and launch a fully qualified executable path with a correctly serialized preview-window handle. Avoid shell command construction. Investigate a Windows Job Object to contain each launched process tree, assigning containment before code runs where supported. Verify behavior when the Windhawk host is already in a job, with both saver architectures, and during abrupt host exit. A process boundary contains many crashes; it is not a sandbox for malicious savers.
+
+Prefer graceful stop, then bounded termination of processes owned by that saver session if needed. Never kill another instance by executable name. A timeout, process exit, or absence of a window does not prove correct visual output; require visible compatibility testing. Cross-process DPI behavior and input routing are feasibility work, not assumptions. See [Microsoft's DPI guidance](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/hidpi/high-dpi-desktop-application-development-on-windows).
+
+## Code reuse and attribution
+
+Use original implementation by default. Reuse a small helper only when its benefit exceeds adaptation/review cost and its provenance and permission are documented. Do not set a percentage target that rewards unnecessary rewriting. Keep a reuse register with source URL, exact commit, original path/symbol, author/copyright notice, applicable license or permission, destination, changes, and validation. Assets and documentation excerpts need the same accounting as code.
+
+| Credit | Relationship | Required treatment |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| [OLED Aegis by spenserlee and contributors](https://github.com/spenserlee/oled_aegis) | Original behavioral reference and potential limited implementation reuse | Acknowledge the project and contributors. Record copied/adapted portions individually. The audit found no license grant in the reviewed repository; resolve source and asset permissions before incorporating them into the new distributable. |
+| [DisplayFusion by Binary Fortress Software](https://www.displayfusion.com/) | Product inspiration and documented preview-hosting precedent | Credit the inspiration and link the technical reference. No commercial source code or assets are proposed for copying; no endorsement claim. |
+| [Actual Multiple Monitors by Actual Tools](https://www.actualtools.com/multiplemonitors/) | Product inspiration for individual display savers | Credit the inspiration and link the compatibility guidance. No commercial source code or assets are proposed for copying; no endorsement claim. |
+| [Windhawk](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk) | Hosting ecosystem and possible integration snippet | Record exact source and applicable terms for any copied wrapper/header/example; retain required notices. |
+| [Monitor Sleep Button by SilverAmd](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk-mods/blob/main/mods/monitor-sleep-button.wh.cpp) | Feasibility reference; optional implementation example | Current source declares MIT. If reused, pin its revision and preserve required notices; otherwise list it as a reference only. |
+
+Phase 0 creates an attribution/reuse register; Phase 6 produces user-facing credits and third-party notices from actual usage. The readme should credit inspiration even if no upstream code ships. Attribution does not replace permission. Calling the result a ground-up rebuild must not imply a clean-room process: the original source has been reviewed. Choose a license for original new work only after the dependency and reuse inventory is known; do not relabel inherited material.
+
+Proposed credit wording, to reconcile with the final reuse register: "Inspired by OLED Aegis by spenserlee and contributors, and the multi-monitor screensaver features of DisplayFusion by Binary Fortress Software and Actual Multiple Monitors by Actual Tools. Built for Windhawk. Reused components and their authors are listed in the third-party notices."
+
+## Implementation phases and gates
+
+Phases are sequential at their gates. Documentation and attribution evolve with implementation. The Phase 1 prototype is intentionally disposable; production structure follows the evidence.
+
+### Phase 0 Requirements and provenance
+
+1. Turn P01-P11 and S01-S08 into a traceable checklist with proposed behavior, legacy evidence, and eventual test evidence.
+2. Curate the existing audit findings with explicit dispositions for the rebuild and retained standalone application. Do not mark a standalone defect fixed just because a new implementation avoids it.
+3. Inventory candidate reusable code/assets, establish permission evidence where reuse is desired, and start the attribution register. Resolve product naming and original-code license before publication.
+4. Record the initial Windows/Windhawk/compiler/hardware test matrix. Proposed primary target is Windows 11 x64; preserve Windows 10 API compatibility where feasible, but advertise support only after testing. Test x86 savers separately on x64 Windows; ARM64 requires its own qualification.
+5. Inventory the stock Windows candidates above without assuming any will pass. Use deliberate missing-file/failed-launch scenarios and record any genuinely incompatible saver as negative cases. Third-party qualification is deferred.
+
+Exit: the feature checklist, rights/reuse inventory, saver candidates, and supported-target proposal are reviewable. Unknown permissions block that reuse, not original implementation or platform research. No product execution is part of this planning document's creation.
+
+### Phase 1 Feasibility prototype
+
+1. Build a minimal tool mod with its own UI loop, tray icon, settings-change path, and reliable unload. Verify the chosen stable compiler with the Win32/COM interfaces the product needs.
+2. Create a host window on a selected display and launch a preview-compatible saver inside it. Then exercise two displays with different savers and two instances of the same saver.
+3. Prove independent input/dismissal and focus behavior. Test mouse movement, click, typing on the other display, media keys, and preview stop. DisplayFusion's own guide reports a mouse-movement dismissal limitation; do not assume our input path solves it without evidence.
+4. Prove cleanup on disable, forced host termination, saver crash/hang, and display removal. Test mixed DPI and x86/x64 child programs. Reject incompatible savers explicitly.
+5. Prove a single canonical settings model can serve Windhawk and any custom monitor/saver picker UI. Settle session pause/exit and startup UX.
+
+Exit: demonstrate P07 plus the essential S01/S02/S05/S06/S07 cases on actual multiple displays, with at least two distinct compatible animated stock savers and a documented failure case. If the mechanism fails, investigate bounded alternatives or revise supported candidates before building the full product. Do not silently remove independent screensavers from V1.
+
+### Phase 2 Production foundation
+
+Implement lifecycle ownership, configuration, monitor identities, controller states, monotonic timing, diagnostics, and a repeatable Windhawk build. Add focused tests for pure policy/configuration logic, including settings failures, all-disabled configurations, stale results, and elapsed-time boundaries. Build failures and absent/stale output must report failure.
+
+Exit: repeated load/settings-change/unload cycles leave no resources behind; configuration and controller tests pass; the build is reproducible on the selected toolchain. Phase 1 experiments have been replaced or reviewed as production code.
+
+### Phase 3 Existing feature parity
+
+Implement black presentation, idle modes, media controls, padding, tray actions, settings UI, legacy import, and startup adaptation. Test P01-P11, explicitly covering all input/media mode combinations. Exercise the maximum polling interval, multi-window browsers, mute state, sleep/resume, and display changes.
+
+Exit: each parity row has evidence or an explicitly accepted behavioral adjustment. Known upstream defects have regression checks or documented non-applicability. This produces an internal parity milestone, not the V1 release.
+
+### Phase 4 Independent screensavers
+
+Integrate the qualified preview-hosting mechanism into the presentation interface. Add per-monitor assignment, preview/configure actions, process containment, launch deadlines, bounded shutdown, and black fallback. Preserve normal display sleep and prevent saver-generated audio from keeping itself or other monitors awake through the mod's media heuristics.
+
+Exit: S01-S08 pass for the initial compatibility list. Unsupported savers receive clear diagnostics. External-saver launch/stop cannot block tray access or leave the user without an emergency dismissal path.
+
+### Phase 5 Reliability and security qualification
+
+Run the full parity and saver matrix on documented Windows/Windhawk targets. Include topology changes while rendering, lock/unlock, sleep/resume, shell restart, audio-device changes, missing paths, Unicode/long paths, denied access, invalid settings, saver crashes, and parent death. Never use normal application runs as substitutes for these failure cases.
+
+Proposed qualification minimum: 50 load/unload cycles and an 8-hour mixed-use soak on the reference system, with no surviving child/window resources after shutdown and no sustained growth in handles, GDI objects, or memory after warm-up. Record host overhead separately from external saver cost. Establish measured CPU/GPU and responsiveness budgets after Phase 1; freeze them before this gate. Validate a bounded emergency-stop response independently of the user-configured polling interval.
+
+Exit: every P/S row has recorded evidence, supported configurations are named, no unresolved crash/data-loss/unload defect remains in release scope, and limitations are written in user-facing language. A security review covers actual new code and reused portions, especially executable launch and shutdown.
+
+### Phase 6 Documentation and release preparation
+
+Prepare installation/removal instructions, settings and startup behavior, migration/import guidance, screenshots made from the finished UI, compatibility entries, troubleshooting, credits, third-party notices, and the license for original code. State supported hosts and dependencies accurately. Pin reviewed CI dependencies and minimize release credentials if CI is introduced.
+
+Verify the distributable compiles in Windhawk from its published source, all required notices travel with it, disable/uninstall cleans up runtime resources, and the original standalone startup entry cannot cause accidental double-running without a documented migration warning. Remove any legacy entry only through an explicit migration action.
+
+Exit: the complete V1 package and evidence are ready for review. Submission to the Windhawk collection, public release, or repository publication is a subsequent explicit action. Collection acceptance is not guaranteed by this plan.
+
+## Risk disposition for the inherited audit
+
+These are planning treatments, not completed remediation or formal audit closeout.
+
