+Configuration maintenance is in scope:
+validated profile export/import, safe per-monitor/all-preferences reset with
+backup, and live status refresh that retains drafts. Keep runtime observations,
+snooze deadlines and fault tickets out of portable profiles. Cross-machine import
+requires explicit monitor mapping and renewed hardware permission; unresolved
+local recovery state survives all resets/imports.
+
+Use an explicit forward schema (normally 3) with schema-2 migration. Keep the
+existing path and stable IDs. Back up schema-2 settings before first successful
+migration; never destroy the only readable copy on a failed save. Existing parser
+bounds, Unicode validation, all-disabled handling and atomic-write guarantees
+must survive. Unknown future schemas fail clearly rather than partially loading.
+
+Older RC4 cannot be assumed to read new settings. Document rollback by restoring
+the pre-migration backup while preserving hardware recovery state separately.
+Do not promise downgrade by simply replacing the source. Validate both forward
+migration and the documented rollback procedure against isolated fixtures.
+
+### Native ownership and recovery
+
+Keep a single controller and one effective configuration per monitor. UI, COM,
+input observation, presentation children, preview and hardware guardian have
+explicit owners and shutdown order. No worker may execute unloaded DLL code.
+
+No UI-thread DDC or slow media/process waits; no emergency lock held across
+external work; no forced thread termination. Existing DDC guardian and fault
+quarantine remain intact. New dim/profile/reset behavior must not bypass them.
+
+Handle topology changes and mixed-DPI settings movement without silently losing
+unsaved edits. Preserve draft state across any intentional window recreation.
+Preview is its own lifecycle; it must not accidentally share production timers,
+hardware operations or per-monitor sessions.
+
+## Execution phases and gates
+
+### P0 — Baseline, design and tooling route
+
+Read and fingerprint the checkout, establish the writer lease, initialize the
+ledger, and dispatch the two bounded W1 reviews if slots are available. Confirm
+the compiler and Fujin pin. Preserve old output directories before doing any
+generation. Settle policy/schema and new output names.
+
+Do not run the current `produce-release.ps1` unchanged: it overwrites RC4.
+Add new output identifiers `nextbeta` and `nextbeta-x64` across affected tooling
+first, or implement an equally explicit versioned-output mapping.
+
+Gate: baseline preserved; requirements mapped; new output route cannot touch
+RC4; key migration/precedence decisions recorded.
+
+### P1 — Explanations and control
+
+Deliver I1/I2/I5/I6, then I3/I4/I7 against the shared policy/config design. Build
+and test the production code with injected observations. Do not bolt UI-only
+countdowns onto logic that cannot explain its own state.
+
+Gate: all I items meet their contracts, old settings migrate correctly, stale/
+ambiguous observations remain conservative, and read-only diagnostics are so.
+
+### P2 — Dimming, controller activity and battery
+
+Implement E1/E4/E5 as adapters and state transitions. Qualify software dim with
+real visible desktop checks only if available and non-disruptive; otherwise
+record the visible/HDR cases precisely while finishing implementation.
+
+Gate: time-bound transitions, input cancellation, noise/held input, power changes
+and topology/session teardown covered through production paths. Old media/input
+behavior remains available.
+
+### P3 — Profiles, preview and native scenes
+
+Implement E2/E3/E6 and reset/import/live-status maintenance. Use the now-stable
+policy/adapter contracts. Keep a simple understandable native UI; preserve
+Fujin and high contrast. The long temporary name must not obscure save/error
+status or create fixed-width layout regressions.
+
+Gate: named profiles and both automatic triggers work; draft preview is contained;
+scene cleanup/resources are bounded; reset/import/rollback protections pass.
+
+### P4 — Adversarial review and corrections
+
+Freeze an exact review source identity. Have W3 inspect lifecycle, config,
+rule precedence, mixed-DPI draft retention, preview and hardware boundaries.
+Lead reproduces findings and fixes relevant defects. Re-review materially
+changed areas. Review is not permission to expand excluded scope.
+
+Gate: no known unresolved critical/high-impact defect in the required feature
+paths; lower-risk limitations are explicit. Do not relabel an unfinished
+required feature as an optional limitation to claim completion.
+
+### P5 — Final build, evidence, package and handoff
+
+Freeze all executable/test/tool inputs. Complete checks on both architectures,
+negative tooling tests and final docs. Package, verify, inspect archive and
+reproduce its source identity. Update ledger/state and final release report.
+
+Gate: completion checklist below passes; installed desktop remains untouched.
+
+## Tooling traps and the required repair
+
+Inspect these files together before changing them:
+`build-production.ps1`, `test-production.ps1`, `diagnose-beta.ps1`,
+`test-beta-tooling.ps1`, `package-beta.ps1`, `verify-beta.ps1`,
+`produce-release.ps1` and `evidence.ps1` under `windhawk/tools/`.
+
+Known traps at preparation:
+
+1. Output ValidateSets stop at RC4; release orchestration hard-codes RC4.
+2. Package expected-version logic derives a 1.0.0 version from directory names.
+3. Archive source filename is selected by exact equality to 1.0.0-rc.4; a new
+   version would otherwise expect the older oled-aegis.wh.cpp filename.
+4. Verifier infers required checks from “-rc.”; other versions omit advanced,
+   power and slides. The new beta must require all existing and new applicable
+   checks, not inherit that weaker historical beta path.
+5. The verifier allowlists only old versions and old required reports.
+6. Exactly eight tooling cases are hard-coded. Preserve those regressions and
+   explicitly version the expected set when adding new ones.
+7. Input inventory hashes every .cpp/.ps1 under windhawk. Any edit there
+   invalidates the build identity; do not repair a receipt to hide it.
+8. Inventory and archive extension filters must be extended if introducing
+   headers/resources or other build inputs. Prefer preserving the single-file
+   source delivery; no invisible generated dependency.
+
+Use an explicit release specification/manifest with required checks, source
+name and reports, validated by the verifier. Do not trust a package-declared
+empty or shortened check list. Preserve verification of existing historical
+archives through explicit legacy mappings. Source/test/tool binding and
+fail-closed evidence checks must become stronger, not looser.
+
+Release identity default: version `1.1.0-beta.1`, output dirs
+`build/windhawk/nextbeta` and `build/windhawk/nextbeta-x64`, archive
+`monitor-screensaver-activity-control-1.1.0-beta.1-source.zip`.
+Preserve the source's existing filename and mod ID. No permanent brand decision
+is being made.
+
+### Intended commands after tooling supports the new output names
+
+These are the target contract, not commands supported by unmodified RC4 tools.
+If a deliberate better interface is implemented, update this block and release
+docs to the actually verified equivalent.
+
+```powershell
+./windhawk/tools/sync-fujin.ps1 -Check
+./windhawk/tools/diagnose-beta.ps1 -OutputName nextbeta
+./windhawk/tools/build-production.ps1 -OutputName nextbeta -Architecture x86
+./windhawk/tools/build-production.ps1 -OutputName nextbeta-x64 -Architecture x86-64
+. ./windhawk/tools/evidence.ps1
+./windhawk/tools/test-production.ps1 -OutputName nextbeta -Checks $AegisChecks -TimeoutSeconds 55
+./windhawk/tools/test-production.ps1 -OutputName nextbeta-x64 -Checks $AegisChecks -TimeoutSeconds 55
+./windhawk/tools/test-beta-tooling.ps1 -OutputName nextbeta
+./windhawk/tools/package-beta.ps1 -OutputName nextbeta
+./windhawk/tools/verify-beta.ps1 -Archive ./build/windhawk/nextbeta/monitor-screensaver-activity-control-1.1.0-beta.1-source.zip
+```
+
+Extend `$AegisChecks` or its versioned successor for any separate new test groups.
+Prefer meaningful additions to existing groups where they fit. Bound individual
+tests; justify any timeout increase with evidence. Do not run two mutation or
+fixture-heavy suites concurrently merely because output paths differ.
+
+Keep an x64 evidence bundle alongside the source ZIP, or include both
+architectures with explicit per-architecture manifests. The final report must
+link both validated identities; a ZIP containing only host receipts cannot
+establish both architectures by itself.
+
+## Verification requirements
+
+Use the same production logic with injected adapters, not a separate toy policy.
+Make fixture identities/events/settings unique so tests cannot affect a loaded
+live mod. Never call real off/lock/OS-setting APIs as a side effect of tests.
+
+Mandatory additions to the existing checks:
+
+- Reasons/countdown: all inhibition reasons, stale/missing data, simultaneous
+  conditions, boundary tick, manual/sticky state and multiple displays.
+- App/input policy: process exits, path/name ambiguity, denied queries, browser
+  processes, muted audio, held keys, pointer/foreground disagreement, window
+  crossing monitors and old combined-local migration.
+- Snooze/profiles: expiry, pause versus snooze, overnight schedules, time jumps,
+  resume, precedence ties, rapid app switching, disabled/disconnected outputs.
+- Rendering/preview: zero/full/intermediate dim, reversal, negative coordinates,
+  mixed DPI, preview closed during launch, hung .scr, invalid photos, two
+  concurrent scenes, no hidden hardware requests.
+- Controllers/battery: deadzones, sustained real input, disconnect/reconnect,
+  missing API, AC/DC/Unknown, transition while launching or cleaning up.
+- Config: valid schema-2 migration, malformed/newer schemas, bounds, Unicode,
+  failed writes, backups, profile remapping, recovery-state preservation and
+  verified rollback fixtures.
+- Diagnostics: bounded buffers, failure paths, redaction and no key/title/
+  screenshot/recovery-secret leakage.
+- Lifecycle/resources: repeated enable/disable/reload/session/topology changes,
+  emergency response during slow work, owned-process cleanup, handle/GDI/thread
+  drift and scene timer stop.
+- Release negative cases: stale/missing/edited receipts, source/input changes,
+  new-beta omission of advanced/power/slides or new groups, wrong source name,
+  malformed/unsafe ZIP, unsupported schema/version and historical archive
+  compatibility.
+
+Run all available existing groups on both architectures after final code/tool
+changes, then packaging rejection checks and final archive verification.
+Do not keep rerunning passing tests without new changes or unresolved concerns.
+If diagnostics/test scripts touch receipts, respect their documented restoration
+and revalidation requirements.
+
+Record separate evidence classes: pure policy, injected platform adapters,
+hidden integration, actual visible desktop, physical hardware. Never infer one
+class from another.
+
+## State, reviewability and interruption recovery
+
+Use [WINDHAWK_NEXT_BETA.md](../plans/WINDHAWK_NEXT_BETA.md) as the authoritative
+feature coverage ledger. The lead alone updates it. Put raw commands/results
+under ignored build output and durable conclusions in WORK_LOG/CURRENT_STATE.
+Add an implementation open loop at execution start; do not reopen old completed
+implementation work or close unrelated standalone audit findings.
+
+At each phase boundary record:
+
+- Exact feature IDs completed/in progress and source/build identity.
+- Tests actually run, failures and evidence paths.
+- Current writer owner and worker assignments/results.
+- Outstanding design decisions and explicit resolutions.
+- Next concrete file/function/action and exact resume command.
+- Whether any final receipts have been invalidated by later changes.
+
+Before compaction, user interruption or execution limit, update this checkpoint.
+Resume from it; do not restart research or ask the operator to restate choices.
+Stop early only for a genuine unresolved authority/data/toolchain blocker after
+finishing independent work, or an execution limit. Report the narrow blocker and
+remaining feature IDs. A partial milestone is not full completion.
+
+## Final completion checklist
+
+- [ ] I1–I7 and E1–E6 implemented and mapped to code/tests in the ledger.
+- [ ] Required config maintenance/migration and rollback procedure verified.
+- [ ] R1–R9 preserved; no excluded O/X/U feature silently added.
+- [ ] Both x86/x64 builds and applicable final test groups pass.
+- [ ] Negative release-evidence tests pass, including the beta-check trap.
+- [ ] Independent review findings resolved or explicit residual defects reported.
+- [ ] Complete standalone source, validated source ZIP and both architecture
+      evidence identities available; historical RC4 archive unchanged.
+- [ ] Current usage, release notes, upgrade/rollback and limitations documented.
+- [ ] Dependency notices, temporary header and technical identities preserved.
+- [ ] Source/ZIP SHA-256 recorded; no unsupported physical-pass claims.
+- [ ] State/ledger accurately describe implementation completion versus
+      outstanding desktop/hardware qualification.
+- [ ] No installation, live OS changes, publication or commit inferred.
+
+Expected report path: `windhawk/docs/NEXT_BETA_REPORT.md`; expected release notes:
+`windhawk/docs/NEXT_BETA_RELEASE_NOTES.md`. These are future deliverables, not
+files claimed to exist at handoff preparation.
+
+Final response should link the full mod, package, report and notes; summarize
+completed features, meaningful checks, actual residual defects and unverified
+hardware cases. Say **implementation complete with desktop/hardware
+qualification outstanding** if that is what the evidence establishes.
