+# Astra Windhawk V1 feasibility handoff
+
+Historical feasibility assignment, completed. D-0007 supersedes its stop point
+and manual-test prerequisite. Continue through the single active
+[production handoff](windhawk-v1-production-handoff.md); do not execute this
+earlier assignment again as the current scope.
+
+Use this document as the starting instruction for the implementing agent. Work in the current OLED Aegis checkout, locally `E:\Citadel\oled_aegis`. Repository ownership and layout are settled in [D-0004](../../state/DECISIONS.md). The [V1 development plan](../plans/WINDHAWK_V1.md) defines the approved scope, acceptance IDs, phases, and attribution requirements.
+
+You are the Astra agent assigned to complete Phase 0 and the Phase 1 feasibility prototype, then deliver the evidence and a recommendation for the production rebuild. D-0006 supersedes D-0005's execution-through-V1 scope. Continue autonomously within this bounded assignment, but stop at the feasibility report even if the gate passes. The operator will decide the next implementation assignment from that evidence.
+
+This assignment starts when the operator gives you this handoff to execute or delegates its execution to you. Production implementation, release preparation, public release, and Windhawk collection submission are outside this assignment.
+
+## Read and establish the starting state
+
+1. Follow [AGENTS.md](../../../AGENTS.md), including the RAIDEN startup reads, [DECISIONS.md](../../state/DECISIONS.md), and [local development rules](../rules/DEVELOPMENT.md).
+2. Read the entire [V1 plan](../plans/WINDHAWK_V1.md), [OPEN_LOOPS.md](../../state/OPEN_LOOPS.md), and the current work log. D-0006 restores a feasibility-only assignment; later phases are context, not current execution scope.
+3. Read the [original README](../../../README.md), relevant [source](../../../src/oled_aegis.c), and the [local audit report](../../../audit-reports/audit-2026-10-04-9581aa1.md). The report and protocol audit ledgers are ignored local files. If absent in another checkout, report that evidence gap and use the tracked plan's finding map to continue independent original-code work; do not invent audit closeout.
+4. Inspect branch, HEAD, remotes, working-tree changes, and available compiler/host tools. Verify that only one agent is writing this repository. Preserve existing planning changes. Do not reset, stash, clean, or discard them to obtain a clean tree.
+
+Use this existing checkout for the handoff: the planning and handoff documents may be uncommitted and therefore absent from a fresh clone or worktree. No new repository, remote, worktree, or history rewrite is needed. Report unexpected conflicting edits and resolve ownership before modifying the affected files; unrelated existing changes are not a reason to abandon the assignment. The managed dirty-target distribution rule does not prohibit ordinary local implementation.
+
+## Repository layout and write scope
+
+Create files as they become useful; this is not a request for empty scaffolding.
+
+```text
+windhawk/
+  README.md
+  prototype/
+    oled-aegis-prototype.wh.cpp
+  docs/
+    ACCEPTANCE.md
+    PROVENANCE.md
+    AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md
+    COMPATIBILITY.md
+    PHASE_1_REPORT.md
+  tools/                       only if a repeatable build/test helper is needed
+build/windhawk/                ignored generated outputs and raw local evidence
+```
+
+Keep the prototype visibly separate from a supported release. A later production assignment may create `windhawk/mods/`; do not begin that work now. Do not introduce a shared library with the standalone application during this assignment.
+
+You may implement and document the Windhawk prototype, add focused tests/helpers, and update Instance-owned continuity files. Keep inherited `src/`, `images/`, root product/build documentation, standalone build scripts, and CI unchanged. Change a repository-wide ignore rule only if needed to keep new generated outputs out of version control. Never write `.raiden/writ/`, central RAIDEN files, or another repository. Do not change remotes, commit, push, submit a mod, or publish a release as part of this assignment.
+
+## Phase 0 deliverables
+
+- **ACCEPTANCE.md:** reference each P01-P11 and S01-S08 requirement from the plan without creating a competing specification. Record planned verification, actual evidence, result, and limitations. Distinguish not run from pass, fail, unavailable, and not applicable with a reason.
+- **PROVENANCE.md:** separate inspiration/references from copied or adapted material. For every reused item, record the exact source revision, path/symbol, author/copyright, applicable license or permission, destination, and modifications. Include assets and integration snippets. Start with original implementation; unresolved upstream rights block that reuse, not independent work. Preserve credits even when no source is copied. Do not claim a clean-room process or manufacture permission.
+- **AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md and continuity:** curate every Medium finding OA-001 through OA-010 into the reserved LOOP-005 through LOOP-014 or an explicit reasoned disposition, with validation criteria. Include the relevant Low findings in the rebuild checklist. Replacing a component in the new edition does not repair or close a defect in the retained application. Do not close LOOP-004 until its actual curation criteria are met; do not rewrite protocol audit ledgers by hand.
+- **COMPATIBILITY.md:** inventory the Windows build, architecture, Windhawk version, compiler, monitor topology/DPI, and stock saver file identities. Exclude serial numbers and private content from committed evidence. The candidate list and qualification rules are in the plan. Test Windows-provided copies; do not download or redistribute savers.
+- **windhawk/README.md:** state that this is an experimental prototype, document the exact build/run/stop procedure, and link the plan and evidence. State dependencies and untested targets plainly.
+
+A supported standalone MSVC build is still tracked separately in LOOP-002. Its absence is not a prerequisite blocking a newly written Windhawk prototype. Verify the Windhawk C++ compiler and required Win32/COM headers independently. Do not install a large toolchain or change global development settings merely to work around an uninvestigated error.
+
+## Phase 1 implementation
+
+Build the smallest original tool mod that can settle the following questions. Use the dedicated user-level Windhawk host, not injection into Explorer or every application. Verify the current stable tool-mod integration mechanism in the official documentation linked by the plan. Inspect the installed version instead of assuming it matches the research snapshot. Account for the source and terms of any integration code copied.
+
+1. Create a tray icon and a properly owned UI/message loop. Provide explicit start-on-selected-monitor, stop-all, and exit/disable handling. Restore the tray after Explorer restarts. Verify unload joins workers and leaves no callback into unloaded code.
+2. Enumerate monitors and create a correctly scaled preview host on the selected monitor. Launch a stock saver with a fully qualified path and properly serialized `/p` parent handle. Preserve focus on the active desktop. Do not use forced reparenting of arbitrary fullscreen savers as the baseline.
+3. Demonstrate that monitor B can render while A remains interactive, then two different savers concurrently, then two instances of the same saver. Exercise the input contract from the plan, including mouse crossing, click, keyboard, and media keys. Verify visible rendering; process creation alone is not success.
+4. Implement a bounded emergency stop independent of the idle polling setting, plus owned-process cleanup. Investigate and verify process-tree containment and host-death cleanup. Close gracefully first; limit any forced termination to processes owned by this prototype session. Never kill a process by saver filename alone.
+5. Exercise missing-file/launch failure, early child exit, an owned-process hang, and mod disable/re-enable. Test mixed DPI and available x86/x64 savers. Report scenarios that need hardware or operator actions, such as display removal, rather than marking them passed.
+6. Prove that Windhawk settings changes and any custom monitor/saver UI can share one configuration model. Resolve the planned session pause/exit and startup adaptation without silently changing global Windhawk or Windows settings.
+
+The first-pass candidates are the stock Windows savers listed in the plan. Qualify every installed candidate; do not substitute third-party savers if a stock candidate fails. Prioritize two animated candidates for the early gate, then record all candidate outcomes. Unsupported preview behavior is useful evidence, not permission to claim compatibility or quietly reduce scope.
+
+## Assess the production decision
+
+Use the experiment to recommend whether to proceed into Phase 2, narrow a compatibility promise, or resolve a specific blocker first. Describe the smallest architecture supported by the evidence, expected areas of reuse, and any changes needed to the V1 plan. Do not silently remove requested features or implement the later phases to work around an unproven premise.
+
+If a feasibility gate cannot pass, investigate and fix what you can within this assignment. Complete useful independent requirements, attribution, documentation, and build work. Request the specific missing evidence or scope decision only when needed; do not mark an unobserved scenario passed.
+
+## Execution and testing boundaries
+
+When executing this handoff, proceed with local prototype source work, builds, focused tests, and reversible feasibility validation. Prefer an existing disposable test environment when it provides the necessary graphics and multiple displays. Desktop tests must have a visible, documented stop path and clean up temporary test installations afterward.
+
+Do not turn on persistent automatic activation, alter OS screensaver/lock/power policies, disable other mods, terminate Explorer, reboot, or put the operator's live desktop to sleep without a task-specific request covering that disruptive action. Use a disposable test environment or request the exact needed manual action after completing independent work. If temporary Windhawk loading cannot be done through available tools or the current permission boundary, finish the build and supply the exact load/test/unload procedure; keep the runtime gate unpassed until actual observations arrive.
+
+There is no requirement to seek confirmation for every implementation choice. Continue through ordinary compiler errors, documentation work, and safe test failures. Ask only for genuinely missing information, unavailable permissions, or an unavoidable disruptive interaction. Do not repeatedly rebuild after unchanged successful checks.
+
+## Feasibility report and stop point
+
+Write `windhawk/docs/PHASE_1_REPORT.md` with the exact build commands/tool versions, artifact paths, per-saver results, actual versus simulated multi-display observations, tray/input/focus/settings findings, owned-process cleanup and containment evidence, reuse status, remaining limitations, and the smallest next experiment for unresolved issues. Conclude with a clear recommendation for the next assignment and whether the Phase 1 gate passed.
+
+Update LOOP-015 and the work log with evidence; keep CURRENT_STATE as pointers to the authoritative records. Do not mark Phase 1 passed unless the plan's gate is met, including two distinct animated stock savers and a failure case on actual multiple displays. A compiled but untested prototype is useful progress but does not pass the runtime gate. Never mark V1 complete during this assignment.
+
+Stop at the feasibility report, or after completing all independent work if an external blocker prevents further feasibility testing. For incomplete work, record exact resumption commands, test environment details, and the smallest action needed from the operator. Return the results and next-step recommendation; leave all work reviewable and uncommitted unless the operator separately requests a commit.
