@@ -0,0 +1,87 @@
+# DAC on Windows ARM64: feasibility report
+
+**Research date:** October 7, 2026 (America/Denver)
+
+**Product baseline:** DAC 0.2.0; public repository commit `9eb03f9cca0f87ac0b1cff3f6cbeb9a1d0595595`
+
+**Scope:** research and source inspection only. No port, installation, compilation experiment, ARM execution, CI change or support-badge change was performed.
+
+## Recommendation
+
+**Yes: Windhawk supports native ARM64 mods, and supporting DAC on Windows ARM64 is a reasonable engineering objective. DAC is not yet qualified for it.** Start with an isolated compatibility investigation on Windows 11 ARM64, then add architecture-aware build evidence and native execution tests. Keep ARM64 outside the advertised support matrix until those gates pass.
+
+Two outcomes must be distinguished:
+
+| Outcome | Meaning for DAC | Assessment |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Runs on an ARM64 PC | DAC may run in an emulated x86 Windhawk process | Plausible on stable Windhawk, but untested; this is not native ARM64 execution |
+| Runs natively as ARM64 | An ARM64 DAC DLL runs inside a compatible native ARM64 tool host | Feasible in principle; host selection/lifecycle and qualification must be addressed |
+| Supports 32-bit ARM | Windows ARM32/Windows RT | No supported Windhawk target found; exclude from the plan |
+
+ARM64 is itself a 64-bit architecture. Our existing “x64” build means AMD64/Intel 64, not every 64-bit CPU. A Windows ARM64 target also does not imply support for Linux ARM, Android or macOS.
+
+## What upstream actually supports
+
+Windhawk **1.6 introduced ARM64 support**, including native ARM64 process customization. Its release notes distinguish that from the limitations affecting emulated x86/x64 processes and warn that individual mods may need adjustment. The GitHub API currently identifies **1.7.3 as the latest stable release**, matching DAC's pinned toolchain. The newest listed prerelease is **2.0.0-alpha.6**, published September 21, 2026. These are separate support tracks. [Windhawk 1.6](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk/releases/tag/v1.6), [stable 1.7.3](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk/releases/tag/v1.7.3), [2.0 alpha 6](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk/releases/tag/2.0.0-alpha.6).
+
+Windhawk distributes the ARM64 engine/toolchain as part of its installation. Local read-only inspection of the installed 1.7.3 tree found:
+
+| Component | Observed location / PE machine |
+| --- | --- |
+| Main executable | `windhawk.exe`: `0x014C` (x86) |
+| ARM64 engine | `Engine/1.7.3/arm64/windhawk.dll`: `0xAA64` |
+| ARM64 link library | `Engine/1.7.3/arm64/windhawk.lib` present |
+| ARM64 C++ runtimes | `Compiler/aarch64-w64-mingw32/bin/libc++.dll` and `libunwind.dll`: `0xAA64` |
+
+Presence and architecture were inspected; loading or executing these components was not tested. The version-pinned upstream compiler supports `aarch64-w64-mingw32` and maps it to the `arm64` engine directory. [Upstream compiler implementation at 1.7.3](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk/blob/v1.7.3/src/vscode-windhawk/src/utils/compilerUtils.ts).
+
+### Metadata is broader than its name suggests
+
+Windhawk accepts `x86`, `amd64`, `arm64`, and `x86-64`. The last is a compatibility selector: on ARM64 it includes ARM64, and can include AMD64 depending on the target processes. DAC's target, `windhawk.exe`, is outside the special system-process list, so the 1.7.3 compiler logic requests x86, AMD64 and ARM64 when ARM64 is enabled and DAC's present metadata is used. **Our existing `x86-64` line is therefore not an ARM64 exclusion.** [Architecture documentation](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk/wiki/Creating-a-new-mod#architecture), [version-pinned compiler selection](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk/blob/v1.7.3/src/vscode-windhawk/src/utils/compilerUtils.ts).
+
+This is a source-derived conclusion, not evidence that DAC compiles or runs on ARM64 today. The local release tooling currently validates only two targets. A future implementation should deliberately choose explicit architecture declarations and align them with its tested matrix; merely adding `@architecture arm64` would not establish support.
+
+## DAC-specific findings
+
+| Area | Evidence in the current repository | Implication / future work |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Tool host | `@include windhawk.exe`; the adapter relaunches that executable, hooks its PE entry point with `Wh_SetFunctionHook`, and uses `CreateProcessInternalW` | Stable 1.7.3's x86 executable implies an x86 tool process under emulation on ARM64. An ARM64 DLL cannot simply replace the x86 DLL in that host. Verify actual launch, hook, callbacks and shutdown on ARM64. |
+| Power helper | Relaunches the current host with `-tool-mod` and DAC-specific power arguments | Any host change must preserve helper dispatch, cancellation, ownership and recovery. Native main-host support alone is insufficient. |
+| Saver paths | `SaverPath` uses `IsWow64Process` to choose `Sysnative` versus `GetSystemDirectoryW` | This older API has documented ARM ambiguities. Use architecture-aware detection and validate actual saver paths/architectures on the chosen OS baseline. Current behavior is not proven broken on Windows 11 ARM64. |
+| Build script | Target validation admits only `host`, `x86`, `x86-64`; PE mappings are `0x014C`/`0x8664`; engine directories are inferred from 32/64 bits | Add an explicit ARM64 mapping: `aarch64-w64-mingw32`, `arm64`, `0xAA64`. Distinguish host architecture, compilation target and execution capability. |
+| Release evidence | `produce-release.ps1`, `release-spec.ps1`, `evidence.ps1`, `archive-evidence.ps1` and rollback scripts encode an x86/x64 pair | Introduce a reviewed multi-architecture evidence contract and rejection fixtures. Do not weaken the current two-architecture verifier or rewrite released historical receipts. |
+| CI | Release workflow runs on x64 `windows-2022` | Cross-compilation can check compilation/linking, but native ARM64 binaries need an ARM64 Windows runner to execute. |
+| Host testing | `platform.cpp` defines `DAC_HARNESS`; the real Windhawk adapter is excluded by `#ifndef DAC_HARNESS` | An ARM64 harness pass alone would not prove the entry-point hook or actual tool-host lifecycle. Add separate actual-host evidence. |
+| Runtime | Standard Win32 UI, GDI+, Core Audio, XInput, monitor enumeration, job/process control and DDC; no DAC inline assembly found in the inspected source | No obvious need for a wholesale rewrite of core policy/rendering. This is a feasibility assessment, not proof of ABI, driver or timing compatibility. |
+
+Source anchors: [metadata and libraries](https://github.com/StarlightDaemon/dac-windhawk/blob/9eb03f9cca0f87ac0b1cff3f6cbeb9a1d0595595/windhawk/mods/dac-windhawk.wh.cpp#L7), [saver paths](https://github.com/StarlightDaemon/dac-windhawk/blob/9eb03f9cca0f87ac0b1cff3f6cbeb9a1d0595595/windhawk/mods/dac-windhawk.wh.cpp#L670), [tool-host adapter](https://github.com/StarlightDaemon/dac-windhawk/blob/9eb03f9cca0f87ac0b1cff3f6cbeb9a1d0595595/windhawk/mods/dac-windhawk.wh.cpp#L2353), [build script](https://github.com/StarlightDaemon/dac-windhawk/blob/9eb03f9cca0f87ac0b1cff3f6cbeb9a1d0595595/windhawk/tools/build-production.ps1), [evidence verifier](https://github.com/StarlightDaemon/dac-windhawk/blob/9eb03f9cca0f87ac0b1cff3f6cbeb9a1d0595595/windhawk/tools/evidence.ps1).
+
+Microsoft recommends `IsWow64Process2` for distinguishing host and guest architectures. Its documentation explicitly describes a Windows 10 ARM case where the older call returns false for a 32-bit application. This warrants a portability change and tests, rather than extrapolating one result across all ARM Windows versions. [Microsoft API documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/wow64apiset/nf-wow64apiset-iswow64process).
+
+The maintainer confirms the 1.x executable is x86 while injected engines match their target processes. Native ARM64 engine support consequently does not make every tool mod native. Emulated-process hooking limitations are a risk to investigate; this research did **not** establish that DAC's specific entry-point hook must fail. [Maintainer explanation](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk/discussions/801).
+
+## Stable versus future host strategy
+
+**Near-term compatibility:** qualify DAC on Windows 11 ARM64 using stable Windhawk 1.7.3, explicitly recording an emulated x86 host if that is what runs. Test installation-time compilation of every target Windhawk requests, not just whichever DLL ultimately loads.
+
+**Longer-term native support:** evaluate Windhawk 2.0's first-class tool-mod host. The current upstream wiki describes automatic `windhawk-mod.exe` hosting and a `windhawk_tool_mod.h` interface. That header is unavailable on 1.7.3. Upstream intends compatibility with the published 1.7.3 snippet, but DAC has a customized adapter and power-helper path, so compatibility must be tested independently. Do not assume that a release note describing a 64-bit service proves native ARM64 execution of DAC's host; inspect the actual selected executable and loaded DLL. [Official tool-mod guide](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk/wiki/Mods-as-tools:-Running-mods-in-a-dedicated-process), [2.0 alpha 2 service change](https://github.com/ramensoftware/windhawk/releases/tag/2.0.0-alpha.2).
+
+Do not upgrade the shipping baseline solely to obtain an ARM64 compiler: 1.7.3 already supplies one. Treat a move to the new host API as its own reviewed change. The operator's no-migration policy permits a clean implementation when chosen; it does not remove host/runtime validation requirements.
+
+## Proposed qualification sequence
+
+1. **Establish the host route.** On a disposable Windows 11 ARM64 environment, inventory OS, Windhawk version, actual host PE architecture and loaded DAC DLL. Verify the existing launcher/entry hook/settings/unload and fake helper lifecycle. This resolves the largest uncertainty before extending release machinery.
+2. **Cross-compile without changing the support claim.** Add isolated ARM64 mod and harness builds against a pinned toolchain; verify imports, PE architecture, runtime dependencies and warnings. A compilation pass is a build result only.
+3. **Execute on ARM64.** Run the applicable policy, storage, lifecycle, process, UI, rendering, version and packaging regressions. Test x86 emulation separately from native ARM64. Preserve historical parser checks only where useful; no migration guarantee is required.
+4. **Validate actual integration and hardware.** Cover native/emulated media apps, raw input, XInput availability, stock/custom savers, photo decoding, mixed-DPI displays, docks/hotplug, AC/DC and suspend/resume. Qualify DDC separately on representative physical monitors, retaining its experimental status if recovery remains uncertain.
+5. **Promote evidence into releases.** Require ARM64 results before advertising it, include per-target receipts in source bundles, and update compatibility docs/badges only for the tested host/OS combination. Publish a new version rather than alter 0.2.0.
+
+GitHub currently lists **`windows-11-arm`** among standard ARM64 Windows runners for public repositories, giving this public project an available native CI path. Runner provisioning and installation must still be tried; hosted machines cannot establish physical monitor/DDC behavior. A managed ARM64 desktop remains necessary for the visible/hardware matrix. [GitHub runner reference](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/runners/github-hosted-runners#standard-github-hosted-runners-for-public-repositories).
+
+## Decision and unresolved questions
+
+Proceed with a future ARM64 investigation, starting with the stable-host experiment. Estimated complexity is **moderate for build/evidence plumbing and potentially higher for host integration**; no defensible calendar estimate exists until the host experiment runs. Core functionality appears portable enough to justify the work.
+
+Open questions are concrete: does DAC's current entry hook work in the emulated stable host; does ARM64 compilation succeed; which architecture does the selected 2.0 host actually run; does the customized helper lifecycle survive that transition; and which saver/audio/display/power combinations work on representative ARM hardware?
+
+Current disposition: **ARM64 feasible, unqualified; ARM32 not planned.** No code or public compatibility claim changed as part of this research.
