@@ -0,0 +1,145 @@
+# Native multi monitor wake latency investigation
+
+The operator confirms the delay occurs in DAC 0.2.1 with native black, clock or
+constellation: one protected display wakes normally, while two can take roughly
+5–15 seconds. The investigation reproduced a missed-wake mechanism in both x86
+and x64 builds. It did not reproduce the operator's physical delay or establish
+which operation stalled on that desktop.
+
+**Confirmed finding:** a mouse event delayed more than 250 ms on DAC's UI queue
+is rejected for independent displays. A simulated 1.5-second filesystem stall in
+the real UI timer path caused the queued wake event to be discarded. Both native
+presentations remained active. A later fresh event immediately dismissed only
+the intended display.
+
+Production code, installation and released assets are unchanged. This is an
+investigation result, not a shipped latency fix.
+
+## What the code does
+
+Reviewed public commit `a09443a060ee257244ea553b7ed880c84d0f7ffa`, source SHA256
+`c5b7a7148b27cbff63acf1e73332ff332ded7ab02951d7c80cfe45bf731281a4`.
+
+- `Input`, source lines 1450–1461, reads the current cursor and foreground state,
+  computes message age, and marks observations older than 250 ms uncertain.
+  `Controller::Activity`, lines 467–475, correctly avoids applying uncertain
+  activity to every independent display. Together these paths discard an old
+  mouse event even if it was the user's only wake movement.
+- The 50 ms timer, lines 2187–2215, shares the same thread as raw input and
+  presentation windows. It checks a power-marker file for every display on every
+  tick, including hardware-disabled displays. With three connected displays,
+  that is nominally 60 attribute queries per second, subject to timer delivery.
+- The same timer calls `Tray()` every five seconds. `Tray`, lines 1470–1484,
+  invokes `Shell_NotifyIconW` on the UI thread even if the icon state is unchanged.
+  Shell and filesystem calls are candidates for instrumentation; neither has
+  been measured stalling on the operator's desktop.
+- The automatic policy check is capped at 250 ms between scheduled evaluations.
+  Raw input directly applies activity and reconciles windows; it does not wait
+  for the configured media polling interval. Increasing polling frequency alone
+  is therefore not a sufficient answer to a 5–15-second wake delay.
+- Native black, clock and constellation create no saver worker or external
+  `.scr` process. Their normal dismissal does not wait through the external
+  saver's 750 ms graceful-close period or two-second process-exit wait. There is
+  no special primary-monitor delay in this path.
+
+The 0.2.1 change intentionally stopped unknown input from waking unrelated displays.
+The queue-age guard existed earlier; the revised unknown-input policy makes lost
+late events a practical wake failure. Removing that guard while continuing to
+read the *current* cursor position would risk attributing an old event to the wrong
+display after the pointer has moved. Restoring a global fallback would revive the
+previous cross-monitor wake defect.
+
+Microsoft documents that [GetMessageTime](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winuser/nf-winuser-getmessagetime)
+describes the queued message's creation time, and that [MSG.pt](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winuser/ns-winuser-msg)
+contains the cursor position when the message was posted. This provides a basis
+for preserving event-time attribution instead of guessing from a later cursor
+sample. [Shell_NotifyIconW](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/shellapi/nf-shellapi-shell_notifyiconw)
+sends a message to the taskbar; its documented API does not establish a specific
+5–15-second timeout. No such timeout is assumed here.
+
+## Reproduction and measurements
+
+The isolated probe includes the unchanged production source with `DAC_HARNESS`.
+It runs the real controller, window creation, input handler, reconciliation and
+UI-owner dispatch, but injects input data, cursor/foreground attribution and message
+time. All presentation windows remain hidden. No physical input, real DDC, visible
+screensaver or live settings are operated. Native scene painting and DWM presentation
+latency on actual monitors are not measured.
+
+Each architecture runs 30 samples for each of black/clock/constellation with one,
+two and three displays: 540 baseline samples in total. The table gives the maximum
+across those display counts in the final run. Activation is controller/window setup;
+wake is handler/reconciliation time after dispatch, not physical input-to-photon time.
+
+| Architecture | Native presentation | Maximum activation ms | Maximum wake handler ms |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| x86 | Black | 0.915 | 0.158 |
+| x86 | Clock | 0.848 | 0.152 |
+| x86 | Constellation | 1.058 | 0.189 |
+| x64 | Black | 0.696 | 0.157 |
+| x64 | Clock | 0.729 | 0.174 |
+| x64 | Constellation | 2.014 | 0.134 |
+
+Fresh events dismissed only the selected display in every sample. Two active
+native sessions did not inherently introduce seconds of cleanup in this harness.
+These timings do not rule out visible rendering, shell or driver delays.
+
+With two black presentations on each architecture:
+
+| Input condition | Result |
+| --- | --- |
+| Event age 100 ms | Intended display dismissed; other display retained |
+| Event age 300 ms | Event ignored; both retained |
+| Event age 5,000 ms | Event ignored; both retained |
+| Injected 1,500 ms delay in a power-marker attribute query on the UI timer | Queued event processed after 1,516 ms and ignored; both retained |
+| Fresh event following that stall | Intended display immediately dismissed; other retained |
+
+The last case demonstrates recovery requires another usable input event; an
+ignored movement is not automatically replayed when the thread recovers. It can
+feel like a long wake delay or a missed wake. The injected file delay is a controlled
+experiment, not evidence that the real filesystem caused the reported incident.
+
+Reproduction source and logs are retained under the public checkout's ignored
+`build/investigation/` directory:
+
+- `native-wake.cpp`: standalone probe including the production source.
+- `native-wake-x64.log` and `.err`: final x64 run.
+- `x86/native-wake.log` and `.err`: final x86 run.
+
+Both builds used Windhawk 1.7.3's clang 20.1.3, C++23, optimization and warnings as
+errors, the production metadata libraries, `-municode`, Unicode defines and Windows
+10 API defines. Targets were `i686-w64-mingw32` and `x86_64-w64-mingw32`; runtime
+libraries came from their matching target directories. Processes ran hidden with
+a 45-second outer bound. Both probes passed all expected-behavior assertions.
+
+## Recommended refinement
+
+1. Preserve event-time monitor attribution and timestamp. Do not silently discard
+   valid mouse wake solely because the UI processed it late. Keep topology/session
+   generations, pre-activation/manual-event rejection, sticky semantics and explicit
+   input scopes. Treat historical keyboard focus separately; do not guess it.
+2. Remove routine disk and shell work from the input-critical path. Cache fault
+   state with background refresh and explicit invalidation on power actions/reset;
+   update tray state on change and use bounded recovery scheduling. Preserve fault
+   quarantine and tray-loss behavior when doing this refactor.
+3. Add bounded latency diagnostics: queue age, UI heartbeat gaps, timer duration,
+   file-query duration, tray-call duration, attribution/rejection reason and
+   reconciliation time. Use display aliases and aggregate counters, not paths,
+   window titles or every mouse packet.
+4. If measured UI stalls remain, separate raw-input collection from presentation
+   and settings work. A collector must preserve per-display observations with bounded
+   storage; it cannot make a blocked UI hide a window by itself. Keep the wake UI
+   path nonblocking too.
+
+Acceptance should include one/two/three native presentations, a single mouse
+movement followed by no further motion, injected 0.3/1.5/5-second stalls, cursor
+crossings, negative monitor coordinates, stale topology, held buttons, keyboard
+focus changes and manual/sticky sessions. No unrelated monitor may wake. Target
+sub-100-ms normal input-to-hide behavior on the actual desktop, with measured
+queue and rendering time reported separately. Under an artificial UI stall, hide
+promptly after the UI becomes available without requiring a second movement.
+
+The operator's actual 5–15-second incident remains open until an instrumented
+desktop run identifies the blocked phase and the refined implementation is retested.
+Hardware power opt-in, system display power, visible rendering and other running
+controllers should be recorded alongside that trace, not presumed responsible.
